  {
   "i": 27,
   "shape_h": "blockquote",
   "shape_m": "blockquote",
   "html_text": "I here propose to do something a little different, namely to employ the ordinary propositional variables ‘ ⟦M⟧ ’, ‘ ⟦M⟧ ’, ‘ ⟦M⟧ ’ etc., for ‘propositions’ in the Diodoran sense [i.e. propositions which ‘may be true at one time and false at another’] and to use certain operators which take such propositions as arguments, and which form functions taking such propositions as values. I shall use ‘ ⟦M⟧ ’ for ‘It will be the case that ⟦M⟧ ’. (1955b: 205.)",
